My first slashem ascension.

Having played three explore-mode games (one to ascension, one until
the game crashed and a lycanthrope until he kept dying consistently
due to having only one Hp, so I gave up...) and after a few (4)
unsuccessful attempts with undead slayers, I decided to try something
simple, a barbarian.  He was able to advance pretty fast but was
stupid enough to pick up a cockatrice in Sokoban, without wearing
gloves...  My second barbarian would presumably have made it, having
defeated Demogorgon already, but the game crashed on him - he "died"
by a trickery.  I say he would presumably have made it, because at the
time I was not fully aware of the dangers of pools created under your
character by a spell; he was still proceeding happily wearing speed
boots and an amulet of reflection, which might have been his demise,
had a pool been created under his feet while he was surrounded by
monsters, on one of the no-teleport levels.

I was pretty frustrated by Bjoerns unfair "death" and decided to
download version 0.0.7E6F3 - those games had been in version
0.0.7E6F2.  My next character was a monk (started on Dec. 31, 2004)
named shui who died from a trickery with a score of only 32781 (on
Jan. 01, 2005), so it seemed that I had gained nothing from the new
version...

I then played around with valkyries on Jan. 1 and Jan. 2.  The
first one was called test and was killed by a unicorn, showing me that
these beasts are not to be underestimated when you are not yet very
fast.  The second died to an elf after having been hit by a sleep ray.
So, apparently valkyries were not easy enough... (in the meantime, I
have wasted five more of them on another computer - in nethack, my
first valk ascended right away :-( ).

In order not to die from a sleep ray again, I fired up a monk named
Luther on Jan. 2.  He ascended on Feb. 28, briefly before
midnight... but he would not have done so, had I not taken some
insurance measure against trickeries.  In fact, I had observed that
the trickeries always appeared on an attempt to change a level, and
almost always on going back up shortly after having gone down a
staircase.  I do this often, if I have to transport many pets down the
staircase and also, because when I see a new staircase, I immediately
go down in order to have knowledge of the position of the new level,
should I fall through a trap door one level higher.  So I go down and
immediately back up again.  The game seemed to crash with a trickery
whenever I changed levels too frequently - no good prospect for a
possible ascension run...  What I did is to save the game after each
change to a new level and to copy the game file to a backup directory,
each time overwriting the old version, so I could fall back only on
the last one.  This way I established my own recovery mechanism, since
the game does not give you the option to recover after a trickery.  On
the ascension run, I saved each time when I had gone up approximately
three levels (which slowed me down considerably in real time), but I
was never "thrown back" there - it seems that the game is reluctant to
do a trickery shortly after resuming from the saved file.

Nevertheless, I had 14 trickeries in the game, 13 standard ones, with a
message like '0.0.7E6F3 20050106: trickery Cannot create file "Klaus
Kassner-Luther.0" for level 0 (errno 13).' in the paniclog file.  The
fourteenth one was different - the game simply froze after I had killed
a minotaur, with his symbol never changing to the one for a corpse, and
no possibility to make any moves.  I killed the game window and tried a
revover, which did work; but when I looked at the playground directory,
I found that file "Klaus Kassner-Luther.1" was missing, and I got
another trickery on trying to save the game again - the last one, by
the way, with the message '0.0.7E6F3 20050220: trickery Cannot open
file "Klaus Kassner-Luther.1" for level 1 (errno 2).'  Fortunately, I
still had the insurance file, which allowed me to resume the game in
exactly the same place where the recovered one had resumed.

So you *might* feel this is not a real ascension, because I had to
recover the game 14 times, but for me it is good enough, since I did
not have any advantages from these recoveries except being able to
continue playing as I would have been in a less buggy situation.  By
the way, I am also playing the game in a Linux environment, and there
I never had the problem.  Therefore, I presume that the problem arises
from the interaction of the game with the operating system, not from a
bug in the game code itself...

Well, back to my character. In my opinion, monks are the easiest class
in slashem, because they can rely on time solving two of the basic
problems of any early character, namely, to get a good AC and to get a
good weapon.  Easy classes, such as the barbarian, tend to have a good
weapon and good stats to begin with, also useful intrinsics (poison
resistance).  The monk has his hands as a good weapon and as he goes
to grand master, martial arts will become as good as any average
artifact or better - this is different from nethack, where a monk will
prefer to use an artifact weapon in the late game.  The slashem monk
does *not* need this, it is even detrimental to his strategy, for he
gains intrinsic AC, as his level raises, up to 11 points, which he
loses every time he wields a weapon, wears body armor other than robes
or wears a shield (and gets back on unwielding or undressing).  Hence,
these items are not useful for the slashem monk but he also does not
need them, due to the power of his martial arts.  Moreover, some of
the techniques allow him to do *insane* amounts of damage, much higher
than that of any of the artefacts, but only for a one-time attack or
at least for a short period of time only.  Afterwards, he will have to
wait until his technique is available again.

Actually, I had wanted to start a human monk, but somehow, he became a
hobbit (I had hit "h" instead of "H"), which I did not like at first,
because he would not be able to go beyond 18 in strength due to that.
Nevertheless, this did not prevent him from fighting very efficiently,
and it turned out to be a major advantage against the demon lords in
the end game...

The first major asset was a cloak of magic resistance that Luther
could buy in an armor shop.  Dungeon town was present in this game on
Dlvl 6, and he could buy a long sword there.  This was dipped into
several fountains until it became Excalibur - Luther was lawful
(figure!).  Of course, he had no intention to use it in regular fight,
being restricted in long sword.  But it was his insurance against
wands of draining.  Monks have the disadvantage that they can get
either permanent reflection or permanent level-drain resistance, by
wearing an amulet, but not both, because they "cannot" get them by
wearing armor.  Temporary level-drain resistance is possible by
wielding Excalibur (and if it is well-enchanted, temporary may last
pretty long :-), but many of the monk-specific techniques remain
unavailable during that time).

Sokoban yielded the amulet of reflection.  Mines end was without a
vampire... (the mimic of mines version).

Dlvl 12 had an altar, so sacrificing was possible from that time on...

Dlvl 13 contained the stairs to Grund's stronghold.  Nobody had a wand
of draining there, so Excalibur was not really necessary.  The throne
gave a wish which went for a bag of holding (I kept getting burdened
with a sack available only.)

At one point, when Luther happened to have four pets, three cats and
one dog, my daughter suggested to change their names to something more
appropriate and so he continued with pets named Mark, Matthew, Luke,
and John. All of them died eventually, their successors had similar
names.  One Luke survived, because he became peaceful after having been
left behind for a long time and I took the time to teleport him out of
the way of my more powerful pets on the ascension runs.

Obviously, I have forgotten many details of the story, so let me
mostly tell you about my major blunders.

After sacrificing some time (and getting the Gauntlets of Defense),
Luther had his first god-sent minion, a monadic deva.  The next zap
with a wand of create monster produced a peaceful hobbit.  Since the
deva killed him anyway, I thought why not offer him on the altar. "Thou
art arrogant, mortal", and, poof, four points of AC were gone and the
altar was changed to chaotic.  Because each time I fired the game up
again, Luther was displayed as an "@", I had finally ceased to activate
the "showrace" option again and had not really appreciated that it was
not humans I was not supposed to sacrifice but hobbits... First I
thought I would not be able to convert this altar again without running
the risk to convert myself.  This was the way I learned, via discussion
with the newsgroup, that using a stethoscope on yourself will teach you
about your alignment.  I was still "piously lawful".  Nevertheless I
decided to go back to minetown first to buy some AC.  After returning I
tried to convert the altar back and was successful.  Then I sacrificed
until Shan Lai Ching was mollified.  (If you needed a mollified god to
begin with, in slashem there appears to always be a coaligned altar -
on the lawful quest.  But one should not try to donate to its priest...
Sacrificing there is o.k., on the other hand.)

On Dlvl 20 were the stairs to the Wyrm Caves, where Luther made tins
of blue and black dragon meat, giving him the last two resistances he
was missing (he got fire resistance from a Xorn on the quest home
level on Dlvl 18, where he had visited only briefly for the time
being, cold resistance was also obtained from eating a Xorn - this was
not at all a vegan conduct game! :-) ).

On Dlvl 22, there was a throne, giving me a genocide, which was used
on master mind flayers.  There was also an altar there and a swamp, a
very useful combination, given that Luther had water walking boots.
At that time, he mostly wore his speed boots, however. Dlvl 23
contained the portal to Ludios, Dlvl 24 was nymphs.  In the meantime,
I had several pet minions, a few monadic devas, a movanic deva, and a
ki-rin. On Dlvl 24, there was also the staircase to the Lost Tomb,
which I cleaned out with relative ease, my minions doing most of the
killing.

The next big blunder happened in the Spider Caves.  First I was
surprised, when going down, to encounter Girtab immediately.  She bit
me, I retreated back up the stairs, and she followed!  Nice.  But while
she could reduce Luther by 60 Hp (from 130), together with one of his
pets, he made short work of her.  Back down.  During most of the time
in the Spider Caves, Luther wore his gauntlets of power instead of the
Gauntlets of defense, because this way he could get rid of most spider
webs.  The big mistake happened when he had already cleaned out the
whole level.  A few webs obviously were still there, and his ki-rin got
caught in one of them.  Well, it should be easy to get him out there
with his strength, so Luther displaced him, tearing the web.  The
ki-rin immediately became hostile, because Luther had scared him!  And
a ki-rin that is not tame is a dangerous foe!  Since Luther had
teleport control (actually this was also a blunder, he had eaten a
tengu out of a whim, and fortunately got teleport control instead of
teleportitis, whereafter he proceeded to eat out in leprechaun
halls...) and could teleport, he escaped to the staircase, where he
hoped to be dealing with the ki-rin on his arrival.  The ki-rin arrived
*very* fast, and started spell-casting, to summon other monsters, among
which a few dragons.  None of the other pet minions attacked him by the
way, as I had hoped (I believe minions of the same god don't attack
each other).  Luther retreated upstairs, the ki-rin followed and hit
him strongly enough to reduce him to 39 Hp.  This was getting tight.
Luther teleported to the upstairs and escaped to the level above,
zapping himself several times with a wand of extra-healing.  39 Hp is
not a good safety margin on Dlvl 23.

Then I started to think.  During the fight on Dlvl 24, I had dropped
my bag of holding to become unburdened.  So there was no way around
it, this ki-rin had to be killed.  In Grund's stronghold, Luther had
gotten a few bullets and an assault rifle; the bullets were enchanted
to +3 to be available against major buddies (at the time I still
thought +2 would be sufficient to hit any monster, which was wrong).
So the plan was obvious - wield the rifle and shoot the ki-rin,
avoiding to shoot my own pets.  The latter was easy, since the ki-rin
followed me immediately when I showed up, whereas my pets lingered.  I
went to the downstairs to be able to escape, if things turned bad.
The ki-rin arrived very fast again, but fortunately did not summon new
monsters that would have been between him and Luther (maybe he needs
to be close enough for that?).  Send first salvo.  "You shoot five
bullets.  The first bullet hits the ki-rin of Shan Lai Ching.  The
second bullet hits... " and so on, all of them hitting.  But he did
not stop approaching!  I took one step back, meaning I would have to
flee by teleportation again, if I could not kill him fast.  But he
fell under the next salvo...  Phew!

On Dlvl 28, there was the staircase to the sunless sea and I knew
already that a magic lamp was waiting there.

Sacrificing had in the mean-time yielded the second monk artifact, the
Holy Spear of Light, which is a very useful item.  Among other things,
it renders a silver saber for Orcus town unnecessary.  On Dlvl 33 were
the stairs to the temple of Moloch, and I tried out the Holy Spear by
invoking it in the hall where all the zombies reside.  Most of them
"burned in the radiance" taking their Hp down so far that my pets and
my monk killed everything easily.  The priests were taken out using a
wand of death - no need to take risks.  In slashem, having four or five
wands of death on the ascension run is quite common, because there are
so many places with opportunities to loot.  The temple of Moloch
yielded another altar to Shan Lai Ching and a lot of spell books,
including poison blast and magic mapping.

At that time (T~50000), I had done only the neutral quest - the easy
one.  Continuing downward found another altar on Dlvl 39, got
Snickersnee on sacrificing, used it wisely by stashing it away.  Of
course, now long sword became unrestricted, so Excalibur was used for a
number of weaker monsters and Luther became skilled to basic in long
sword.  (He was a grand master in martial arts and did not advance any
other combat skills, only spell skills.)

He went down to Dlvl 41, which was Medusa's, without a titan.  Then he
started preparing for the chaotic quest by alchemizing 9 potions of
full healing and blessing them.  He drank the first, a djinni appeared
and granted him a wish - a bit unexpectedly.  He drank the second,
another wish!  So now he had a ring of levitation and an amulet of
magical breathing.  (He had a robe of power since Moloch's temple and
the only major piece of armor missing was a Hawaiian shirt which he
got from Ludios by polypiling later and fixating it using a potion of
restore ability.  I wonder what the message will be, if you don't
fixate it, when it returns to its initial form, be that a helmet or a
cloak.)  The other 7 potions just gave 8 additional Hp each.  Then he
went back up to Dlvl 18, to deal with master Kaen.  With an AC of -35,
this seems to be no big deal, but Luther was overprepared.  He quaffed
a potion of invulnerability, wasted a charge from a wand of death on
Kaen and forgot to try out the wand-of-sleep approach.  So there Kaen
was, Luther was on the stairs and invulnerable.  Time for some
measurements.  Only 137 Hp, hmm.  No need for sophisticated
tactics. The (uncursed!)  potion of invulnerability will last for
about 10 turns, plenty of time.  A few martial arts stances and Kaen
was dead.  The Eyes of the Overworld and a certain silver bell
belonged to Luther.

Next came Dlvl 17, Vecna.  For him, a special strategy was needed. All
L were already genocided (blessed scroll), so the idea was to get in
there, sneak up on him, build a fort using a scroll of earth and shoot
him while he can't do anything to the PC.  Well, I could not get at him
while he still was meditating, since the vampires and zombies on the
level woke him up.  When he teleported next to Luther, the latter
teleported away to an open place that I had planned to use before and
read his blessed scroll of earth.  Vecna was hit by a boulder but was
still next to the player and hit him.  So a scroll of scare monster had
to be dropped.  Vecna wandered away and could now of course not
approach anymore.  Shoot.  "The first bullet hits it.  The bullet does
not seem to harm it. ..."  What?  They were enchanted to +3!  On my
last encounter with Vecna, I had shot him with +4 bullets.  But +3 is
not sufficient.  I had left my bag of holding, in which there was
indeed another scroll of enchant weapon, outside of the magic portal...
So that meant I had to expose myself.  Well, at least I had a potion of
invulnerability, this time a blessed one.  Luther teleported out after
quaffing it, to the magic portal.  Vecna followed immediately and
summoned a few monsters, one of which was a purple worm, blocking the
portal.  I did not want to go through anyway, because Vecna would have
followed me, so I decided to fight, being safe for a few turns. "You
start bashing Vecna with your assault rifle."  Merde!  Unwield weapon,
start martial arts.  The purple worm swallowed me, but since I was an
only slowly digestable entity, he did not like me and left me alone for
a turn. When the purple worm finally moved away from the portal, I
could have gone through, but Vecna was still next to me. Two options
were now available, teleport him away or continue hitting him - he most
certainly did not seem to want to go away by himself.  I decided first
to check his Hp.  Only 18 left. Well, attack him with Chi strike, so he
does not get a turn to flee. His body crumbled to dust, leaving his
precious hand behind.  I had already half physical damage via the
Gauntlets, but regeneration is not bad either...  Also, had my monk
been neutral, this would have been his emergency exit against wands of
draining (without the option to continue fighting, however)...  As it
happened, the guild of disgruntled adventurers was not present in this
game, so wands of draining remained scarce.  Orcus had one, but no
opportunity to use it, and they did not reappear before the Astral
Plane.

Finally, the lawful quest was done, since Luther now had a means of
levitation.  Nightmare was no trouble and the amulet of flying of the
Spice girl mind flayers was also retrieved.  The throne of Nightmare
gave a genocide, which I used on disenchanters, because there were no
mind flayers anymore and L were gone, too.  Because my unicorn horn was
at +7 already, I left Nighthorn there, realizing only much later that
it would be an emergency source of reflection just as Excalibur is an
emergency source of drain resistance.

Down to the Castle on Dlvl 42 and major blunder number three.  This
was mostly due to the fact that I had some eight pets now - devas and
a ki-rin again.  When I had already gained the entrance (after blowing
up the drawbridge) I retreated again, because the soldiers kept
throwing frag grenades at me and I needed my pets out of the way to
dance back whenever a frag grenade arrived at my position.  Out I
went, took off the ring of levitation and battled a few monsters.  For
some reason, I took a step towards the castle entrance again without
putting on the ring first.  Fell into the moat.  Now this is not quite
the same thing as falling into the castle moat in nethack (which never
happened to me): "Falling into the sparkling waters of Lethe, you
forget all your mundane concerns..." or something similar.  Holy shit!
I got out, of course, after an unsuccessful attempt at teleportation,
but as I suspected, this was a major catastrophe, not a minor one. One
thing foreboding disaster was the fact that I had forgotten all of the
level.  My AC had gone up from -45 to -42, several pieces of outer
armor having lost one point of enchantment.  Most of my potions in the
bag of holding had turned into uncursed water or potions of amnesia,
including all potions of full healing and the two potions of
invulnerability.  I was in no shape of facing Demogorgon.  Most of my
scrolls were blanked, including 14 cursed scrolls of teleportation
that I had made after producing unholy water via the confused-remove
curse trick.  I saved the game and put it in the backup directory,
just to make sure not to get into temptation :-) ... Potions of
amnesia act similar to mind flayer attacks in slashem when thrown at
you and will make you forget maps; when you drink them, I suppose you
will forget even more, but that is easy to avoid as they are always
sparkling.  But now I had taken a bath in one big potion of amnesia!
Moreover, it was difficult to replace things, because I had looted
already most of the dungeon and only left the garbage behind.  (One
potion of holy water and a few scrolls of remove curse in my oilskin
sack had survived.)  Apparently, I had not forgotten any discoveries,
however, nor spells.

I moved back up into the dungeon, leaving my pets behind - my magic
whistle had become a tin whistle and my magic candle an ordinary
candle, but I knew there were duplicates lying around in the dungeon.
My keys and many other items had become rusty, even inside the bag. The
about 50 +5 bullets I had prepared had turned into rusty +4 bullets;
the rifle, at +0 had only become rusty.  In any case, this accident
would delay my ascension by several thousand turns.

My first plan was to get items by pudding farming.  I kicked several
sinks and attacked the arising puddings wielding a rusty +0 bullet. The
puddings first multiplied but obviously a +0 bullet is still to
ferocious a weapon - they were all killed after some tens of them had
appeared.  None had dropped a single item, so I suspect that in slashem
pudding farming is impossible.  I am not sure however - the count of
killed puddings after the ascension is only 62 black ones and 11 brown
ones, so I may not have been patient enough.

Another alternative seemed to be to kill One-eyed Sam.  Due to the
overpriced nature of his/her articles, I had refrained from buying,
although I had seen some nice items in the shop (no magic lamps
though).  I hoped to get potions there, for scrolls I could always fall
back on magic markers, using one or two wishes from the castle wand
which was (0:1).  Being lawful, I fought long with my conscience.
Finally, I decided to try a more tedious approach of stealing with the
help of pets.  I could have killed him without losing telepathy for a
long time, as I verified that he had blessed potions of ESP in his
shop.

I payed his shop a visit, zapping a wand of create monster in the
hallway outside the shop proper and killing the arising monsters until
a dog appeared that I tamed with a tripe ration.  I continued zapping
until I had a cat, too.  During the time that followed, two more cats
were created randomly, so I ended up having four pets.  First, I just
let them pick up potions that I had piled near the shop entrance and
whistled them out of the shop with my newly acquired magic whistle.
Next I dropped and sold gems to Sam.  He pays 2000 $ per diamond, of
which I had 8 and he paid regular prices for my 3 sapphires and 14
emeralds as well.  Dropping all of these would give me some 30000 $,
sufficient to pay for three or four potions.  My cats and dog would
retrieve the gems.  At one point, I had 80000 $ that burdened me, so I
decided to drop them in the shop far from the entrance (pets will only
carry ten times their level in gold, so it is no use having them pick
up the gold directly) and to sell gems only for credit to Sam (he had
no money left - the 80000 $ were my credit but stayed on the floor
after having been spent), which gave slightly lower rates but still
enough to stay in business.  I bought or "acquired directly" all the
potions in the shop and the useful scrolls, quite a number of enchant
armor, enchant weapon, remove curse and also two scare monster.  Those
I had picked up only when I had some 30000 $ credit.  They were
uncursed but did not disintegrate - apparently the first pick-up is
free...  The whole procedure did not seem to take long; at least the
*felt* number of turns was not excessive :-). I parted from Sam being
on friendly terms with him, leaving my pets in his care (they had
killed Izzy the Rhaumbusun and Daphne the nymph, but Sam did not seem
to mind)...

Since I was burdened, I put all the potions that so far were useless
into my stash on Dlvl 22 (right above Sam), next to the altar and the
swamps.  Here was my basic idea:  I wanted potions of invulnerability,
I had two potions of restore ability.  So I would first collect as many
potions as possible and then polymorph potions and scrolls until I got
the potions of invulnerability which I would then fixate in their
polymorphed form by dipping them into the potion of restore ability.  I
also hoped to get some potions of restore ability on polymorphing which
after fixating I might use to keep any useful scroll that I got by
polymorphing in its new form.  Potions of extra healing or full healing
I would quaff more or less immediately, because they would not stay in
their new form forever.  Most of the plan worked, I ended up having
five fixated potions of restore ability and 21 scrolls of enchant
weapon, but...

Before doing this, I back-tracked through all of the dungeon.  After
all, I had forgotten many levels that had to be remapped (mostly using
the magic mapping spell), and I wanted *all* the potions above the
castle, to have enough material to polymorph.  I went even down to the
bottom of the mines, where I recovered (on Dlvl 7) a spellbook of charm
monster, which I took along as it might come in handy to be able to
make friends on the ascension run.

Unfortunately, no potion of invulnerability turned up on my polymorph
attempts.  On the other hand, a glass golem did appear.  Well, so I
would have to do Demogorgon with a scroll of scare monster instead... I
turned the remaining garbage potions into water.  Two of the uncursed
potions of water did not stack with the others, so I supposed they were
still "hazy" and put them in my stash box.  Later, I noticed they had
turned into a potion of blindness and one of hallucination, remarkably
those potions that you can turn into water without diluting them in a
moat or cancelling them.

Luther used his six scrolls of enchant armor to get his AC to -50.
Three items were only enchanted to +4, so the maximum without
exchanging the robe by a robe of power would have been -53.  But I
thought, -50 would do nicely.  A decision about armor had to be made at
that point, because I had read about a tragic death of one would-be
ascendee on the Plane of Earth, due to a pool created below him.  This
essentially meant to either levitate of fly on all no-teleport levels
or to wear water-walking boots, losing some of Luther's speed.
Levitation was inacceptable, because it would make the gun unusable,
due to the recoil (only one bullet will be shot in each round).  Flying
would have meant to forgo reflection.  Well, choices of this kind are
one of the essences of nethack and slashem.  I decided to go using
water-walking boots and to rely on the haste-self spell for speed most
of the time.  I also took along 5 blessed potions of speed, in case
spell-casting would be impractical and speed essential nonetheless.

Some of the 21 scrolls of enchant weapon were used to get the Holy
Spear to +7, the rifle (a new one, without rust) to +5, and to acquire
about 300 blessed +7 bullets, of which Luther could take along only one
