This was already on the Plane of Earth.  On Dlvl 1, Luther had shifted
several useful items from his bag of holding which during almost all
the time after acquiring the Amulet was in his oilskin sack, to the
sack, including a potion of invulnerability, one of full healing, four
scrolls of gold detection and several scrolls of charging. There were
already two potions of holy water in the sack. A few K-rations were
moved outside the bag, too, as well as the amulet of magical breathing
and one of his four amulets of life saving, and Luther quaffed four
out of his five potions of speed (which would turn out to keep him
very fast for the remainder of the game), to lose some weight.

Earth was easy, as Luther avoided fighting whenever possible.  The
Eyes allowed to see earth elementals when they were still two squares
away, so this determined the path to the portal that Luther chose.  He
put on the ring of levitation before going through the portal and
whistled his pets through the portal.  He had five of them now, three
monadic devas besides the Archon and Planeter, having picked them up
again on his way up.

Air was easy.  It seems that air elementals don't do that much damage
to a character with AC -50, after all.  Luther killed one by martial
arts from the inside, the others usually via a wand of death.  Having
four wands of death and five blessed scrolls of charging, economizing
with charges was not necessary.

Fire was easy.  No powerful monster appeared, I had hoped for a
Planetar or even a Solar (who would most likely have been peaceful
anyway), having level 30.  By the way, all experience levels but three
were gained by killing monsters, and the three gained by potions were
intermediate ones, not the last ones.  Before moving through the
portal, Luther swapped the amulet of reflection for that of magical
breathing.  No reflection from now on for the rest of the game.

Water was easy.  I don't genocide ; anymore, because they are a source
of food on water where you usually stay an indeterminable period of
time.  But Luther had genocided electric eels, so no worries for his
rings or wands.  The only problem on water was to avoid to go through
the portal accidentally without my pets close by.  But I detected it
before it was covered by an item, so that worked out, too.

On Astral, Luther switched to an amulet of life saving, just to make
sure he could survive the first hit from one of the named characters
wielding Vorpal Blade or the Tsurugi.  He counted mostly on using a
potion of invulnerability and kill the opponent by martial arts during
the 12 to fifteen safe turns the potion would give (more than 30
opportunities to hit in blink mode).  He determined that Famine was on
the left, Pestilence in the middle and Death on the right.  Actually
Pestilence was coming out the door, so he turned left and whistled his
pets close (one monadic deva had been killed on Fire, not being fire
resistant, so the number of pets was still five).  The Eyes which he
had put back on turned out extremely useful, because he got a message
while still not having opened the first door "Maud the <rank name>
wields a long sword named Vorpal Blade".  Don't recall what it was, a
healer or a wizard or a tourist.  Anyway, that meant to be careful.
He used charm monster to tame ants and similar critters created by
opponents reading scrolls or zapping wands, so he could relatively
easily block the spaces around him with pets.

When the door was opened, Luther managed to first get his Planetar in
contact with Maud, who could not harm the Planetar with Vorpal Blade,
from which I concluded that it cannot be enchanted strongly.  But in
the end it turned out to be at +8, sufficient to hit any monster
easily!  This must have to do with the fact that monsters fighting
each other are treated by a different algorithm than monsters being
fought by the PC.

Luther quaffed the first potion of invulnerability and proceeded,
because the Planetar was making slow progress only.  "Maud ... zaps a
wand of draining. Goodbye level 30."  Arghh.  The situation was now
slightly inconvenient.  To protect myself against the wand, I wielded
Excalibur.  But that meant that I could not wield my rifle for Famine
and it also meant that I hit with less than the maximum possible at
Maud.  Actually forgetting that Excalibur will prevent my monk attack
techniques doing anything, I tried chi strike and had to hit Maud
twice before I noticed that I got no chi strike message.  I had
anticipated a means against any of the three attacks, that of Famine,
that of Vorpal Blade, and that of a wand of draining, but these means
were incompatible with each other, so an attack of all three at the
same time was difficult to counter.  So I tried a wand of death, which
missed Maud but killed some other critter, getting my level back to 30
again.  The best would now have been to unwield Excalibur and just hit
Maud (or even shoot her, wielding the gun), she could hardly have
taken more than a few levels from me and Vorpal Blade was not a danger
for a few more turns.  She might have worn a cloak of magic
resistance, in which case my wand of death attacks would have been
useless.  But the second one hit, and it did kill her.  It also hit a
certain "Steve the Voyager" and made him angry.  Because I know that
on Astral it can easily happen that you kill a formerly peaceful human
by accident, I had brought two blessed potions of ESP to get telepathy
back, just in case.  However, for the time being I merely teleported
Steve away.  I switched to the rifle, just in time, because Famine
moved next to me.  Actually, I had planned to let neither of the
riders get that close.  But of course I shot him in the next turn,
using up five bullets.

Ran to the altar, leaving my pets behind to deal with the resurrecting
Famine (they must killed him two more times).  The altar was neutral,
I believe, in any case it was wrong.  But in the temple, Luther had
time to take the second potion of invulnerability out of the bag of
holding (and put it in the sack), to replenish his bullet supply and
to take out two more wands of death (he had thrown away enough empty
wands to be not burdened by this anymore). He also put Vorpal Blade
into the bag of holding, never to use it.

Back to the center, shooting Famine in passing.  This time, he could
not approach closer than to two squares distance, because the room
contained very few monsters only.

The idea was to try the right altar (Death) next.  But when
approaching, I realized that Pestilence actually had wandered towards
the right, leaving the middle altar unguarded.  So I went in there and
managed to get to the altar, before Pestilence could catch up.  Had to
teleport Steve out of the way again.  Pestilence approached and there
was a free gap of three squares to him, so I just shot him.

Then I needed some whistling to do in order to get those pets that I
wanted to go with me to the demigod bar around the altar (I had well
above 8 pets right now).  I succeeded to get the Archon, the Planetar,
and the Angel of Shan Lai Ching next to me, while the (remaining?)
monadic deva was two squares away.  I had the maximum possible 7 pets
about me (one square was occupied by the high priest) but only four of
them are mentioned by the game - apparently ants don't really count.

